From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] wowprospero: see a new 32-bit thread arrive, or not arrive.

The question that found the thread bug. winmm's devices thread was never
running a guest instruction -- the unix side's "translator starting",
logged once per thread that enters pw_wow_run, fired only for the main
thread -- and nothing said how far up the thread got before stopping.

BTCpuThreadInit is where wow64 first shows the CPU backend a new 32-bit
thread, and it was an empty function, so nothing was visible at that layer
at all. With these two lines the answer was immediate and decisive:

    BTCpuThreadInit: 1       (the main thread, at process start)
    simulate entry: 29       (all of them thread 0024)

No new thread ever reached wow64, which put the fault below it, in ntdll's
own thread startup -- and from there in the request pipe the client closed
out from under the server (wine patch 0866).

ERR rather than TRACE so they arrive with no channel enabled, and one line
per thread: BTCpuSimulate is entered once and then looped in.

⚠ Keep them. Between these and "translator starting" the three gaps a
thread can die in are distinguishable -- before wow64, between wow64 and
the backend, and inside the translator's own per-thread setup.
diff --git a/wine/wowprospero/cpu.c b/wine/wowprospero/cpu.c
index 549dbd4..1f8f465 100644
--- a/wine/wowprospero/cpu.c
+++ b/wine/wowprospero/cpu.c
@@ -112,6 +112,16 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI BTCpuProcessInit(void)
 
 void WINAPI BTCpuThreadInit(void)
 {
+    /* ⚠ DIAGNOSTIC, at ERR so it arrives without a channel.
+     *
+     * GTA IV hangs because winmm's devices thread never runs one guest
+     * instruction: WINMM_StartDevicesThread creates it and waits on
+     * {event, thread handle} forever, the wait never returns, and the unix
+     * side's "translator starting" -- logged once per thread that enters
+     * pw_wow_run -- fires only for the main thread. The thread exists and has
+     * not died. The question is how far up it gets, and this is the first
+     * place the CPU backend sees a new 32-bit thread at all. */
+    ERR( "new 32-bit thread\n" );
 }
 
 void WINAPI BTCpuThreadTerm( HANDLE thread, LONG status )
@@ -426,6 +436,12 @@ void WINAPI BTCpuSimulate(void)
 
     C_ASSERT( sizeof(ctx->ExtendedRegisters) == sizeof(fp) );
 
+    /* ⚠ DIAGNOSTIC, with BTCpuThreadInit's. Entered once per thread and then
+     * looped in, so this is one line per thread, and it says whether a thread
+     * that reached the backend also reached the translator -- "translator
+     * starting" comes from the unix side of the very first pw_wow_run below. */
+    ERR( "simulate entry, eip %#lx\n", (unsigned long)ctx->Eip );
+
     for (;;)
     {
         /* The Unix side reloads the complete context on every entry, which
